<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="chp2sec12" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

    <title>Dimension</title>
    <p>
        Throughout this subsection, <m>V</m> will denote a vector space over the field <m>F</m>.
    </p>
    <definition xml:id="def-finitedimensional">
        <title>Finite Dimensional</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If there exists a <em>finite</em> subset <m>S\subset V</m> such that <m>S</m> spans <m>V</m>, (i.e. <m>\spn(S)=V</m>), then <m>V</m> is <term>finite dimensional</term>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>
    <theorem xml:id="thm_ind_less_span">
        <title>Independent sets are no larger than spanning sets</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>V</m> is finite dimensional, then the size of an independent set is less than or equal to the size of any spanning set of <m>V</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>
    <proof>
        <p>
            Try a direct proof.
            <m>I</m> could potentially be either finite or infinite.
            It suffices to prove the result for <m>I</m> finite (why???).
            Suppose that <m>I=\{\vect{u}_1,\ldots, \vect{u}_i\}</m> is an independent set and <m>S=\{\vect{v}_1,\ldots, \vect{v}_s\}</m> is a spanning set.
            Your goal is to show <m>i\le s</m>.
            Try to produce a set <m>S_i</m> such that <m>I\subset S_i</m> and <m>\spn(S_i)=\spn(S)=V</m>.
            You might be able to do this with an iterative process that I will now suggest.
        </p>
        <p>
            Consider the set <m>S_1':=\{\vect{u}_1\}\cup S=\{\vect{u}_1,\vect{v}_1,\ldots, \vect{v}_s\}</m>.
            Deduce <m>\spn(S_1')=V</m>.
            Deduce <m>S_1'</m> is dependent.
            Deduce there exists some <m>\vect{v}_{j_1}\in S_1'</m> such that <m>\spn(S_1')=\spn(S_1'-\{\vect{v}_{j_1}\})</m>.
            Let <m>S_1:=S_1'-\{\vect{v}_{j_1}\}</m>.
            Observe <m>|S_1|=|S|=s</m>.
            Appropriately iterate this strategy, creating <m>S_2'</m>, <m>S_2</m>, <m>S_3'</m>, etc. until you are left with the desired <m>S_i</m>.
            Justify why this process terminates after you have put all of <m>I</m> into <m>S_i</m>, and not before.
            You may find <xref ref="def-finitedimensional"/>, <xref ref="prop-dependence-prop2"/>, <xref ref="thm-rra"/>, and <xref ref="prop-dependence-prop3"/> useful.
        </p>
    </proof>

    <insight>
        
    <p>
        <xref ref="thm_ind_less_span"/> is often called the <q>first nontrivial result</q> in axiomatic linear algebra.
        While that may be a little too strong, its proof is different that those of previous results.
        Analyze the proof of <xref ref="thm_ind_less_span"/> and over the span of several paragraphs, carefully identify and describe features (strategies, arguments, ways of thinking, etc.) that are unique to it.
        Do you agree that <xref ref="thm_ind_less_span"/> is the first nontrivial theorem we have encountered? Explain?
    </p>
    </insight>

    <corollary xml:id="cor_dimension_well_defined">
        <title>Independent spanning sets have the same cardinality</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>V</m> is finite dimensional, and <m>S_1</m> and <m>S_2</m> are linearly independent spanning sets, then <m>|S_1|=|S_2|</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </corollary>
    <proof>
        <p>
            Try using <xref ref="thm_ind_less_span"/>… twice.
        </p>
    </proof>
    <definition xml:id="def-dimension">
        <title>Dimension</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>V</m> is finite dimensional, and <m>S</m> is an independent spanning set, then the <term>dimension</term> of <m>V</m> is <m>|S|</m>.
                In symbols, we write <m>\dim V:=|S|</m>.
                If <m>V</m> is not finite dimensional, we write <m>\dim V =\infty</m>.
                The dimension of the trivial subspace is taken to be 0.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>
    <proposition xml:id="prop_dimension_of_lists">
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>n\in \Z_+</m>, then <m>\dim F^n=n</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </proposition>
    <proof>
        <p>
            Try a direct proof.
            Find a set of <m>n</m> vectors that are independent and spanning.
            Be sure to verify they satisfy these conditions.
            You may find <xref ref="def-vector_space_of_lists"/>, <xref ref="def-dependence"/>, and <xref ref="def-span"/> useful.
        </p>
    </proof>

    <insight>
    <p>
        Dimension is a word that invokes geometry.
        In what ways is this geometric invocation useful and appropriate?
        Might this geometric concept image lead you astray in certain contexts?
        In the metaphor of mixture, what does dimension mean?
    </p>
    </insight>

   

</section>








